## Context

Child of 0700. Replaces the former tracker 2044 (closed as superseded by this ticket and 2048). The safe part: build the improved dedup keys as machinery, with the production pool output unchanged until 2048 flips the switch.

Read-only audit of 2026-10-09: the pool rebuilt from the pinned catalogue and the archived lane deliveries gives 389,291 works from 426,636 rows (matches the 2026-10-01 baseline). `_rel_pool_dedup.cluster` is order-independent (two shuffles, seeds 1 and 2, identical partitions). Defects found, all in the identity of a work:

1. `utils.normalize_title` (pipeline_text.py) lower-cases and deletes `[^\w\s]` only. Observed: `Emerging markets&rsquo; carbon pricing` gives `emerging marketsrsquo carbon pricing`; `Post-Kyoto` gives `postkyoto` but `Post Kyoto` gives `post kyoto`; `<i>Climate</i> finance` gives `iclimatei finance`; the NFC and NFD forms of `légitimité` give different keys (`légitimité`, `legitimite`). It unescapes no HTML entity, strips no tag, folds no hyphen, does not normalize Unicode.
2. Under a stricter title key used for the audit (unescape, strip tags, NFKD, drop combining marks, casefold, punctuation to space), 5,600 title and year groups hold 14,921 works. 1,034 of those groups have at least one work with no DOI (corrected 2026-10-09: this text first said "no DOI and no OpenAlex id"; the 2047 coder measured 785 groups without two distinct DOIs and 704 with an identifier-less work, and could not reproduce 1,034 under either reading): the candidates for an under-merge without a DOI conflict. Another 4,566 groups have distinct DOIs (some are OpenAlex duplicate W ids, unverified). 1,357 adjacent-year pairs with an identifier-less side are not joined.
3. 365 works carry 2 or more DOIs and 59 carry 2 or more OpenAlex ids, joined through a shared OpenAlex record (SSRN and Research Square preprints with the published article). The module docstring says a working paper and its article with their own DOIs stay two works; the author decided on 2026-10-09 that they are ONE work (the rule itself is 2048).
4. The RePEc handle is not a dedup key: 62,726 works have only a title key. Ticket 2040 now delivers a verified `repec_handle` column for EDS records; the RePEc mirror lane (t1810) carries the handle in `record_id`.

Labels are keyed by `work_key` and are append-only (ICF screen table, stage-1 and stage-2 labels). A change to the normalizer or the keys re-keys `title:` and `url:` works; 2048 handles the labels.

Safe means: with the default (`dedup_version` 1) the pool is byte-identical to today's, no `work_key` changes, no label is touched, nothing waits on another branch. With `--dedup-version 2` the build writes a report and a migration table but replaces nothing.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] A new title normalizer in its own tested module: HTML entities unescaped, tags stripped, NFKC, hyphens and dashes to space, casefold, whitespace collapsed; no ASCII folding that collides non-Latin scripts. Accent folding is decided by measurement on the clean candidate groups of 2044 (the 1,034 groups with no identifier conflict) and stated; default none. Every observed string of 2044 Context item 1 is a test, plus a script-collision test (distinct CJK, Cyrillic and Arabic titles never share a key).
- [ ] `repec_handle` (ticket 2040) and the RePEc handle carried in `record_id` by the RePEc mirror lane become an exact-equality join key, joining only components whose DOIs and OpenAlex ids do not disagree, with the same chain protection as the Handle step (2b).
- [ ] A `dedup_version` setting (config, default 1). Version 1 is the current cascade untouched; version 2 uses the new normalizer and the handle key. A test proves the pool built with version 1 is byte-identical to the pool built before this change on the same inputs (`SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH` set), including `work_key`.
- [ ] `--dedup-version 2` also writes, outside the pool: a report of the works that would merge by cause (normalizer, handle key), the multi-DOI and multi-OpenAlex works, the title-only works remaining, and an append-only migration table `old work_key to new work_key` for every work whose key would change. Nothing in the production pool or any label table is rewritten.
- [ ] Counts of the report by lane, period (1990-2006, 2007-2014, 2015-2025) and language, with the first act shown separately.
- [ ] Order independence kept for both versions: a shuffle test with a positive control (a deliberately order-dependent variant fails it).
- [ ] The docstrings of `_rel_pool_dedup.py` and `_rel_pool_keys.py` are accurate for both versions: they state the normalizer and the handle key of version 2, say that version 1 is still the default, and record the author decision of 2026-10-09 that a working paper and its published article are ONE work with the pointer that rule lands in 2048. No sentence contradicts the code.
- [ ] Surface sweep, from the repo and not from the diff: list every title-normalizing call site that feeds a dedup or a join (the repo has at least four `normalize_title` definitions: `scripts/pipeline_text.py`, `scripts/_rel_toc_core.py`, `scripts/qa/qa_bib_doi.py`, `scripts/qa/qa_metadata.py`), say for each whether it shares the weaknesses of Context item 1, and decide reuse, replace or leave, with the reason. The new normalizer must not become a fifth copy: the pool path and the TOC lane use one definition, or the ticket log states why not.
- [ ] Diff at most 14 content-bearing paths; scoped gate quoted (`selected:` and `skipped:`). If the sweep makes the diff exceed 14, the sweep's replacements go in a follow-up ticket and this ticket keeps the machinery and the list.

## Notes

Severity: science lane (it builds the fix for a live defect that reaches the pool). Do not run in parallel with 2045 on `scripts/corpus_rel_pool.py`; 2045 goes first, rebase between them.
